<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:avc="using:Avalonia.Data.Converters"
             xmlns:controls="using:GalactiLog.App.Controls"
             xmlns:dash="using:GalactiLog.App.ViewModels.Dashboard"
             xmlns:cc="using:GalactiLog.App.ViewModels.CustomColumns"
             xmlns:settings="using:GalactiLog.App.ViewModels.Settings"
             xmlns:q="using:GalactiLog.Data.Queries"
             xmlns:views="using:GalactiLog.App.Views.Dashboard"
             x:Class="GalactiLog.App.Views.Dashboard.TargetListView"
             x:Name="Root"
             x:DataType="dash:TargetListViewModel">
  <!-- design-spec 12.2's target list. An ItemsControl and a header row rather than a DataGrid:
       Avalonia's DataGrid is a separate package that Directory.Packages.props does not pin, and
       dynamic column visibility plus a per-row expander plus tinted badge cells are more work to
       bend a DataGrid into than to lay out directly (coordinator ruling Q7).

       Phase 14C, user ruling U3: flat rule-separated rows on one Grid.IsSharedSizeScope spine
       (DESIGN.md section 6, "The aligned table"), one line high, the sessions control at the row's
       trailing end. The header grid and every row grid declare the same seven columns: Name is the
       one star column, with a 120 floor and its own computed cap, and the other six are Auto and
       shared by SharedSizeGroup, so each is as wide as its widest cell across the header and every
       realized row. The seven fixed TargetColumn*Width resources, the per-row card Border, the
       leading "n sessions" toggle, the stacked common name and both WrapPanels are retired with it.

       The row fits its viewport at every width down to about 680 and is never carried by a
       horizontal scroller (fix pass, review C1); see the ScrollViewer's own comment below for why
       the scroller has to be disabled rather than merely hidden, and the header grid's for why the
       header is drawn at a row's own width.

       The 8 pixel cell gutter is carried inside the cell as padding (Padding="8,0" on a row cell,
       the same figure on a header cell's own Button), never as a margin and never as the grid's
       ColumnSpacing: as a margin it fell outside MaxWidth, so a capped row cell measured 16 wider
       than its header cell and the shared group took the larger of the two; and ColumnSpacing
       would leave a hidden column's gap behind, where a hidden column has to collapse to zero
       width in the header and in every row together. A header cell's IsVisible sits on its
       ContentControl, not on the button inside it, for the same reason (review item 2).

       Cells bind ColumnViewModel.IsShown rather than IsVisible (Phase 6 Task 5), so
       the dashboard and the frame table render through one property. The dashboard has no metric
       groups, so IsGroupEnabled is always true here and the two are the same value; the column
       picker still binds IsVisible, which is the persisted list.

       Nothing here sets FontSize. Scales.axaml's FontSize* keys are ratios (0.5 to 0.714), not
       point sizes, so binding one to FontSize renders sub-pixel text; the whole list inherits the
       root size until Phase 9 lands general.text_size (review item 1). Every colour is a theme token (spec 14) except a palette badge's tint,
       which is the configured filter colour and therefore user data. -->
  <UserControl.Resources>
    <!-- The trailing inset. Task 5 makes the themed vertical scrollbar an overlay that takes no
         layout space, so the thumb would otherwise draw over the trailing 8 pixels of a row, which
         is where the Expand button sits. One value, used by the header grid and by every row grid,
         so the two cannot be inset differently and drift apart. Phase 14C Task 4's pager reuses the
         same key so its own trailing edge lines up with the header row's own trailing edge, the
         column gear (fix pass, task4-review.md P3: measured on the launched application, the
         gear sits at the same x the pager's select does, while the Expand buttons sit 8 pixels
         short of both, the header-versus-row gap that is Task 3's region and not this one's). -->
    <Thickness x:Key="TableTrailingInset">0,0,12,0</Thickness>

    <!-- One header cell: a button that sorts, showing the direction glyph on the active key. A
         column with no sort mapping (designation, palette) still renders as a button; the command
         ignores it, which is one branch in the view-model instead of two header templates.

         Classes="header-cell" carries the alignment and the cell declares Padding="8,0" itself,
         which is the spec's cell gutter since the second fix pass; the base Button style's own
         padding is not what carries it. Background and BorderThickness are still set here:
         Button.header-cell holds the two alignment setters and nothing else, so without these two
         attributes every header label would draw inside an outlined 1 pixel button box, which is
         the same pair Views/TargetDetail/FrameTableView.axaml's own header cell sets. -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="TargetHeaderCell" DataType="dash:ColumnViewModel">
      <Button Classes="header-cell"
              Command="{Binding #Root.((dash:TargetListViewModel)DataContext).SortByCommand}"
              CommandParameter="{Binding Key}"
              HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
              HorizontalContentAlignment="Stretch"
              Background="Transparent"
              BorderThickness="0"
              Padding="8,0">
        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto">
          <TextBlock Grid.Column="0"
                     Text="{Binding Title}"
                     FontWeight="SemiBold"
                     TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                     Foreground="{DynamicResource ColorTextSecondary}" />
          <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                     Text="{Binding SortGlyph}"
                     Margin="4,0,0,0"
                     VerticalAlignment="Center"
                     Foreground="{DynamicResource ColorAccent}" />
        </Grid>
      </Button>
    </DataTemplate>

    <!-- The same cell with Button.header-cell.numeric over it, for the two right-aligned columns
         (spec 12.2, "Column order and alignment"). Two templates rather than a
         Classes.numeric="{Binding IsNumeric}" binding: the dashboard's six ColumnViewModel objects
         are built with isNumeric left at its default in TargetListViewModel.cs, which is Task 4's
         file this phase, and the header grid already names its six cells one by one.

         The direction marker leads the label here rather than trailing it. A right-aligned cell's
         label has to end exactly where its column's figures end, and a trailing marker puts its
         own width, and the panel's spacing even when the marker is empty, between the two: measured
         on the launched application, "Integration" sat 4 to 8 pixels inside its own column's
         values. Leading, the label's trailing edge is the column's trailing edge whether the
         column is the sort key or not. -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="TargetHeaderCellNumeric" DataType="dash:ColumnViewModel">
      <Button Classes="header-cell numeric"
              Command="{Binding #Root.((dash:TargetListViewModel)DataContext).SortByCommand}"
              CommandParameter="{Binding Key}"
              HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
              HorizontalContentAlignment="Stretch"
              Background="Transparent"
              BorderThickness="0"
              Padding="8,0">
        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*">
          <TextBlock Grid.Column="0"
                     Text="{Binding SortGlyph}"
                     Margin="0,0,4,0"
                     VerticalAlignment="Center"
                     Foreground="{DynamicResource ColorAccent}" />
          <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                     Text="{Binding Title}"
                     FontWeight="SemiBold"
                     TextAlignment="Right"
                     TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                     Foreground="{DynamicResource ColorTextSecondary}" />
        </Grid>
      </Button>
    </DataTemplate>

    <!-- One filter badge, shared by the Palette column and the session line's Filters cell
         (spec 12.2: both cells draw the same Border.tag badge), so the two agree by construction
         rather than by two copies of the same markup.

         Border.tag from Theme/Controls.axaml carries the chrome: ColorBadgeBg at radius 3, padding
         6,1. Two things are set here. The filter's configured colour is the badge's 1 pixel
         outline, over Border.tag's own ColorBorderDefault, which is the idiom
         Views/Dashboard/FilterPanelView.axaml's filter pill already uses, so the dashboard's two
         filter surfaces agree; and the label is ColorBadgeText, a theme token, because the tint is
         user data with no contrast floor against a theme background and a stored #4040FF measured
         about 1.5:1 on the dark themes (coordinator ruling, fix pass, reversing question Q2). The
         frame count is on the tooltip rather than inside the badge, which is what kept the cell to
         one line (user ruling U3 item 4). The tint is parsed by TargetRowViewModel.ParseTint
         (FIXER F10, F29) and is the one non-token colour in this view. -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="TargetFilterBadge" x:DataType="dash:PaletteBadgeViewModel">
      <!-- 2026-10-05, user request: the web app's frosted-glass badge. The outer Border keeps
           Border.tag's chrome (ColorBadgeBg base, 1 px edge, radius 3) with the edge and shadow
           re-tinted from the view model; the inner Border is the tint wash inside the edge, which
           is the one way to layer a translucent tint over a themed base without a literal. The
           label is the tint, as on the web, which reverses the ColorBadgeText ruling above. -->
      <Border Classes="tag" Margin="0,0,6,0" Padding="0"
              BorderBrush="{Binding GlassEdge}"
              BoxShadow="{Binding GlassShadow}">
        <Border Background="{Binding GlassFill}" Padding="6,1">
          <TextBlock Text="{Binding CanonicalName}"
                     FontWeight="SemiBold"
                     Foreground="{Binding Tint}"
                     ToolTip.Tip="{Binding FrameCountText}" />
        </Border>
      </Border>
    </DataTemplate>

    <!-- Spec 12.15's custom column strip. One header label and one editor per drawn column, both
         at the same fixed width, so the header strip and every row strip hold the same number of
         entries at the same widths and line up by construction; the DashCustom shared size group
         then makes the two grids agree on the strip's total.

         The width comes from TargetListView.CellWidth over the column's name and type and the
         view's own type size and family, never as a literal: View_NoCellDeclaresAFixedWidth fails
         any Width="<digit>" in this file and is what stopped the seven retired fixed cell widths
         coming back. The figures are declared once, in TargetListView.axaml.cs beside the other
         caps, and the entry is as wide as its heading when the editor needs less, so a check box
         column's name is read rather than drawn as an ellipsis. Above the cap the heading trims
         and the tooltip and the automation name carry the whole of it.

         The header cell carries no sort button: a custom column is not sortable in this phase,
         which is what keeps the header's own button count at six. -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="TargetCustomHeaderCell" x:DataType="q:CustomColumnDefinition">
      <TextBlock Text="{Binding Name}"
                 ToolTip.Tip="{Binding Name}"
                 AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding Name}"
                 Padding="8,0"
                 FontWeight="SemiBold"
                 VerticalAlignment="Center"
                 TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                 Foreground="{DynamicResource ColorTextSecondary}">
        <TextBlock.Width>
          <MultiBinding Converter="{x:Static views:TargetListView.CellWidth}">
            <Binding Path="Name" />
            <Binding Path="Type" />
            <Binding Path="#Root.FontSize" />
            <Binding Path="#Root.FontFamily" />
          </MultiBinding>
        </TextBlock.Width>
      </TextBlock>
    </DataTemplate>

    <!-- One cell, on a target row and on a night line alike. The editor declares no width, no
         margin and no background of its own (Task 3), so the gutter rides on this container, which
         paints nothing: a press in the gutter belongs to the row's own hit area, while a press on
         the editor is swallowed by the control and never opens the target. -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="TargetCustomCell" x:DataType="cc:CustomValueViewModel">
      <Border Padding="8,0" VerticalAlignment="Center">
        <!-- The same converter over the same column, so a cell is exactly as wide as its own
             heading entry and the two strips line up by construction. -->
        <Border.Width>
          <MultiBinding Converter="{x:Static views:TargetListView.CellWidth}">
            <Binding Path="Column.Name" />
            <Binding Path="Column.Type" />
            <Binding Path="#Root.FontSize" />
            <Binding Path="#Root.FontFamily" />
          </MultiBinding>
        </Border.Width>
        <controls:CustomCellEditor />
      </Border>
    </DataTemplate>
  </UserControl.Resources>

  <Grid RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto,*" Grid.IsSharedSizeScope="True">
    <!-- The pager (spec 12.2 Pager paragraph, Phase 14C Task 4), above the header row, where the
         count is read before the rows rather than at the bottom (questions.md Q5, which is why
         PageStatusText stays and stays rendered here). Spans the list's own width and carries the
         same TableTrailingInset as the header and every row grid, so its trailing edge lines up
         with the header row's own trailing edge, the column gear (fix pass, task4-review.md P3;
         see TableTrailingInset's own comment above for the measured figures). Replaces the old
         build's bottom Previous and Next pair, which carried no page numbers and no page-size
         control.

         Three columns, not two (launched-app look): Previous, the page numbers and Next are
         Auto, and spec 12.2's own pageRange rule caps that set at seven slots whatever the total
         page count, so the column is inherently bounded. PageStatusText gets the middle column,
         and only that column is starred: a bare TextBlock in a star column measures within the
         width the Grid actually offers it and trims there, where a StackPanel does not, because
         a horizontal StackPanel's own MeasureOverride offers each child infinite width regardless
         of what its own parent offered it (the same "a star column does not shrink below its
         cell's desired width" finding TargetListView.axaml.cs's own NameCellMaxWidth remarks
         record for the row grid, traced here to its root cause). The page-size select stays Auto
         on the trailing end. -->
    <Grid Grid.Row="0"
          x:Name="TargetListPager"
          Margin="{StaticResource TableTrailingInset}"
          ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto">
      <StackPanel Grid.Column="0"
                  Orientation="Horizontal"
                  Spacing="6"
                  VerticalAlignment="Center">
        <Button Classes="sm" Content="Previous" Command="{Binding PreviousPageCommand}" />

        <!-- The web's own pageRange rule (TargetFeed.tsx), transcribed in
             TargetListViewModel.BuildPageButtons: one button per page number, and the ellipsis a
             non-interactive text run of three ASCII full stops rather than a Button (task4.md
             section 6). Neither carries a TargetRowViewModel as its CommandParameter, so a page
             click can never be mistaken for a row click. -->
        <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding PageButtons}">
          <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
            <ItemsPanelTemplate>
              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="4" />
            </ItemsPanelTemplate>
          </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
          <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
            <DataTemplate x:DataType="dash:PageButtonViewModel">
              <Panel>
                <TextBlock Text="..."
                           IsVisible="{Binding Page, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNull}}"
                           Padding="6,0"
                           VerticalAlignment="Center"
                           Foreground="{DynamicResource ColorTextTertiary}" />
                <!-- Button.page.current (Theme/Controls.axaml, Phase 14C Task 4) marks the current
                     page; Button.sm keeps every page button the pager's own row height. No new
                     button style lives here: both classes are the shared vocabulary's.
                     GoToPageCommand carries no CanExecute (TRACKING item 13: the gate is the
                     body's, past a click that reaches it directly), so the numbered buttons grey
                     through a direct IsEnabled binding instead (fix pass, task4-review.md P3),
                     the same affordance NextPageCommand and PreviousPageCommand now get through
                     their own CanExecute. -->
                <Button Classes="sm page"
                        Classes.current="{Binding IsCurrent}"
                        IsVisible="{Binding Page, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}"
                        IsEnabled="{Binding #Root.((dash:TargetListViewModel)DataContext).IsRefetching, Converter={x:Static avc:BoolConverters.Not}}"
                        Content="{Binding Page}"
                        Command="{Binding #Root.((dash:TargetListViewModel)DataContext).GoToPageCommand}"
                        CommandParameter="{Binding Page}" />
              </Panel>
            </DataTemplate>
          </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
        </ItemsControl>

        <Button Classes="sm" Content="Next" Command="{Binding NextPageCommand}" />
      </StackPanel>

      <!-- questions.md Q5: the count read before the rows is the Pager paragraph's own reason
           for the move, so PageStatusText stays and stays rendered; here it is the one element
           that gives way first, since the lit page button already carries the current page.
           Fix pass (task4-review.md P2): the text itself is now the range alone ("1 to 25 of
           120"), the web's own line, rather than repeating the page count the buttons beside it
           already show; a trailing margin keeps a trimmed run clear of the select even when the
           leading one alone would not. -->
      <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                 Text="{Binding PageStatusText}"
                 Margin="8,0,8,0"
                 VerticalAlignment="Center"
                 TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                 Foreground="{DynamicResource ColorTextSecondary}" />

      <!-- Spec 5.8.1's four choices, writing general.default_page_size on selection through
           TargetListViewModel.SelectedPageSize. An unlisted stored value reads as the select's
           own default, 50 (NormalizePageSize), rather than clamping to the nearest option. -->
      <StackPanel Grid.Column="2"
                  Orientation="Horizontal"
                  Spacing="6"
                  VerticalAlignment="Center">
        <TextBlock Text="Rows per page"
                   VerticalAlignment="Center"
                   Foreground="{DynamicResource ColorTextSecondary}" />
        <ComboBox ItemsSource="{x:Static dash:TargetListViewModel.PageSizeOptions}"
                  SelectedItem="{Binding SelectedPageSize}" />
      </StackPanel>
    </Grid>

    <!-- Header row, under its own 1 pixel rule. The cells are bound one per column so each can
         carry its own visibility; the column order is DisplaySettings.DefaultColumns["dashboard"],
         which is what the view-model builds Columns in. -->
    <Border Grid.Row="1" Classes="rule">
      <!-- The seven columns, in the order DisplaySettings.DefaultColumns["dashboard"] declares.
           The seventh is the sessions control's own cell and is not a persistable column key
           (spec 5.8.2's default list holds six): the control is always present, exactly as the
           leading toggle cell was, so its header cell carries no sort button.

           The header grid is drawn at a row's own width, not at a row's width less the help glyph
           and the gear: a star column resolves to whatever is left, so a header measured 60 pixels
           narrower than a row would put every column after the star out of line with its cells.
           The glyph and the gear are therefore drawn right aligned inside the header's own empty
           seventh cell rather than in two grid columns of their own. That cell is as wide as the
           Expand button below it, which is wider than the pair.

           Name is the one star column and carries no SharedSizeGroup, because a shared size group
           cannot be star sized. It still aligns with its cells: every row is drawn at the same
           width, so the star resolves to the same width in every row, and every other column is
           shared. This is the Nights ledger's own rule and its own comment
           (Views/TargetDetail/TargetDetailView.axaml, "Avalonia's Grid does not shrink an Auto
           column when the arranged width is smaller than the sum"), applied to the second table
           that needed it rather than re-derived, and it is the whole of review finding C1 and C2:
           seven Auto columns summed to about 990 inside the 720 the page gives the list, so
           Equipment was cut, Last Session and the sessions control were off screen, and at a wide
           window the same seven hugged the left with dead space after them.

           Name's floor is 120, which holds a catalogue designation plus its two gutters, and it
           carries NameCellMaxWidth as well, the share left once every other shown column has taken
           its own cap: a star column in Avalonia does not shrink below its cell's desired width,
           so bounding both the header cell and every row cell by one computed figure is what stops
           the two grids resolving different column sets.

           Every Auto column but Palette carries a cap, declared once in TargetListView.axaml.cs
           and applied here through DataCellMaxWidth and EquipmentCellMaxWidth rather than as a
           literal on a cell: an Auto column is resolved before a star, so an uncapped one can
           still take the whole row and leave the star at its floor, which is what the fixer pass
           closed (phase-review P2, four of the five caps the fit width was built from reached no
           cell). Palette is the one left uncapped, because question Q10 rules that no badge is
           clipped and the badge count is not capped, so its figure is a budget in the arithmetic
           and nothing in the markup. -->
      <Grid x:Name="TargetListHeaderRow" Margin="{StaticResource TableTrailingInset}">
        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
          <ColumnDefinition Width="*" MinWidth="120" />
          <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" SharedSizeGroup="{Binding #Root.DesignationSizeGroup}" />
          <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" SharedSizeGroup="{Binding #Root.((dash:TargetListViewModel)DataContext).Columns[2].SizeGroup}" />
          <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" SharedSizeGroup="{Binding #Root.IntegrationSizeGroup}" />
          <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" SharedSizeGroup="{Binding #Root.EquipmentSizeGroup}" />
          <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" SharedSizeGroup="{Binding #Root.LastSessionSizeGroup}" />
          <!-- Spec 12.15: ONE grid column for every custom column, not one each. Avalonia cannot
               bind a ColumnDefinitions collection (AVLN3000, recorded in
               TargetDetailViewModel.cs), so the strip is an ItemsControl of fixed-width cells
               inside a single Auto column. It measures nothing at all while no custom column is
               switched on, which is every library until the reader switches one on. -->
          <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" SharedSizeGroup="DashCustom" />
          <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" SharedSizeGroup="DashSessions" />
        </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
        <ContentControl Grid.Column="0"
                        MaxWidth="{Binding #Root.NameCellMaxWidth}"
                        HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                        IsVisible="{Binding Columns[0].IsShown}"
                        Content="{Binding Columns[0]}"
                        ContentTemplate="{StaticResource TargetHeaderCell}" />
        <ContentControl Grid.Column="1"
                        MaxWidth="{Binding #Root.DataCellMaxWidth}"
                        HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                        IsVisible="{Binding Columns[1].IsShown}"
                        Content="{Binding Columns[1]}"
                        ContentTemplate="{StaticResource TargetHeaderCell}" />
        <ContentControl Grid.Column="2"
                        IsVisible="{Binding Columns[2].IsShown}"
                        Content="{Binding Columns[2]}"
                        ContentTemplate="{StaticResource TargetHeaderCell}" />
        <ContentControl Grid.Column="3"
                        MaxWidth="{Binding #Root.DataCellMaxWidth}"
                        HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                        IsVisible="{Binding Columns[3].IsShown}"
                        Content="{Binding Columns[3]}"
                        ContentTemplate="{StaticResource TargetHeaderCellNumeric}" />
        <ContentControl Grid.Column="4"
                        MaxWidth="{Binding #Root.EquipmentCellMaxWidth}"
                        HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                        IsVisible="{Binding #Root.IsEquipmentShown}"
                        Content="{Binding Columns[4]}"
                        ContentTemplate="{StaticResource TargetHeaderCell}" />
        <ContentControl Grid.Column="5"
                        MaxWidth="{Binding #Root.DataCellMaxWidth}"
                        HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                        IsVisible="{Binding Columns[5].IsShown}"
                        Content="{Binding Columns[5]}"
                        ContentTemplate="{StaticResource TargetHeaderCellNumeric}" />

        <!-- The custom column labels, one per drawn column, at the same widths the row cells
             carry. No sort button and no picker of its own: the column gear beside it is what
             switches a custom column on and off. -->
        <ItemsControl Grid.Column="6"
                      x:Name="CustomHeaderStrip"
                      VerticalAlignment="Center"
                      ItemsSource="{Binding DrawnCustomColumns}"
                      ItemTemplate="{StaticResource TargetCustomHeaderCell}">
          <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
            <ItemsPanelTemplate>
              <!-- A StackPanel, never a WrapPanel: both WrapPanels are retired and a wrapped
                   strip would make the row two lines high again. Declared rather than left to the
                   default so a later reader does not have to know what the default is. -->
              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" />
            </ItemsPanelTemplate>
          </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
        </ItemsControl>

        <!-- The header's last cell: the sessions column's own, with no sort button in it, and
             the list's two page controls drawn right aligned inside it so they cost the header no
             width of its own. -->
        <StackPanel Grid.Column="7"
                    Orientation="Horizontal"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                    VerticalAlignment="Center">
          <!-- Spec 12.12's dashboard.targets. The list has no heading, so the glyph sits on the
               header row beside the Columns button rather than on a column header, which the
               placement rule forbids. -->
          <controls:HelpButton Topic="dashboard.targets" />

          <!-- Column picker: a gear rather than a text label (spec 12.2, ruling D4). DESIGN.md
               section 8 and spec 14.5 refuse a Unicode glyph standing in for an icon (questions.md
               Q10), so this draws a Path over Theme/Controls.axaml's IconGear StreamGeometry, the
               same idiom IconHelpRing/IconHelpMark already use, rather than typing a character. The
               tooltip keeps the word the button used to show as its label. Each checkbox in the
               flyout still raises ToggleColumn rather than writing IsVisible itself, so the
               persisted list and the checkboxes cannot disagree; name's is disabled (ruling Q5).
               This flyout, the live Columns list and ToggleColumnCommand it binds are already the
               same instance AppHost.cs hands the Display tab's picker (ruling D4's "one
               ColumnPickerViewModel instance shown in two places"); this button is the re-skin, not
               new plumbing. -->
          <Button x:Name="ColumnGearButton"
                  Padding="8,3"
                  ToolTip.Tip="Columns">
            <!-- Phase 14B fixer, fixer list item 41: Path.icon-glyph from Theme/Controls.axaml,
                 which is the one declaration of these four stroke setters and carries the
                 pointer-over ink this icon had none of. The 16 square here is the icon's own size,
                 not a cell width: it is the one Width in this file and
                 View_NoCellDeclaresAFixedWidth allows it by name. -->
            <Path Width="16"
                  Height="16"
                  Classes="icon-glyph"
                  Data="{StaticResource IconGear}" />
            <!-- Spec 12.15: custom columns are listed under a "Custom" heading below the built-in
                 ones, so the flyout draws the picker's own groups rather than the flat column list.
                 The picker is a view over this same live collection and this same toggle, so there
                 is still one dashboard column state (ruling D4), and the split is the picker's one
                 implementation rather than a second one written here. ShowGroupHeadings is false
                 while only one group exists, which is every library with no target-scope custom
                 column, so the flyout of such a library is exactly what it was before Phase 20. -->
            <Button.Flyout>
              <Flyout>
                <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding ColumnPicker.Groups}">
                  <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                    <DataTemplate DataType="settings:ColumnGroupViewModel">
                      <StackPanel Spacing="2">
                        <TextBlock x:Name="ColumnGroupHeading"
                                   Text="{Binding Heading}"
                                   Margin="0,4,0,0"
                                   IsVisible="{Binding #Root.((dash:TargetListViewModel)DataContext).ColumnPicker.ShowGroupHeadings}"
                                   Foreground="{DynamicResource ColorTextTertiary}" />
                        <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Columns}">
                          <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                            <DataTemplate DataType="dash:ColumnViewModel">
                              <CheckBox Content="{Binding Title}"
                                        IsChecked="{Binding IsVisible, Mode=OneWay}"
                                        IsEnabled="{Binding CanHide}"
                                        Command="{Binding #Root.((dash:TargetListViewModel)DataContext).ToggleColumnCommand}"
                                        CommandParameter="{Binding}" />
                            </DataTemplate>
                          </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                        </ItemsControl>
                      </StackPanel>
                    </DataTemplate>
                  </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                </ItemsControl>
              </Flyout>
            </Button.Flyout>
          </Button>
        </StackPanel>
      </Grid>
    </Border>

    <!-- The horizontal scroller is disabled, not hidden: a ScrollViewer that can scroll sideways
         measures its content at infinite width, so a star column inside one then resolves to its
         content's desired width rather than to the viewport, so nothing would ever give way and the
         scrollbar would be the only outcome. Disabled, the row is measured at the viewport and the
         two star columns trim.

         The header sits outside this scroller and used to drift out of line with the rows once they
         were scrolled sideways (review P3). That is unreachable now: there is no horizontal scroll
         at any width at or above the row's own minimum, about 680 for the list's own viewport.
         Below that the trimming has run out; the trailing cells clip rather than scroll, and
         restoring a scroller for that case alone would bring the desync back with it. -->
    <ScrollViewer Grid.Row="2" HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled">
      <!-- The refetch dim (spec 12.2, "The refetch dim", Phase 14C Task 4): while a filtered query
           is in flight, the rows already on screen stay rendered, dim and stop taking input; Load
           still replaces nothing until the new page lands (TargetListViewModel.Load is untouched),
           so the dim is the only signal this adds. IsHitTestVisible carries the input-disable half,
           not IsEnabled: an IsEnabled="False" subtree drops keyboard focus in Avalonia and never
           restores it, which failed the case that proves focus already inside a row survives the
           refetch, and the web's own treatment is pointer-events-none, which is a hit-test rule
           rather than a disablement of the element. Classes.refetching carries the dim, its Opacity
           setter on ItemsControl.refetching in Theme/Controls.axaml rather than inline, and never
           inside a Button or ToggleButton style block (ControlStyleScanTest's gradient and fill
           scan). -->
      <ItemsControl x:Name="TargetListRows"
                    ItemsSource="{Binding Rows}"
                    IsHitTestVisible="{Binding !IsRefetching}"
                    Classes.refetching="{Binding IsRefetching}">
        <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
          <DataTemplate DataType="dash:TargetRowViewModel">
            <!-- One bottom edge per row, never a top edge as well: two neighbouring rows would
                 otherwise draw a 2 pixel line between them. The sessions expander opens inside
                 this pair, under the cells and above the bottom edge. -->
            <Border Classes="rule">
              <StackPanel>
                <!-- Row height 34 as a MinHeight, not a Height: a row grows if a theme type does,
                     it does not clip.

                     The row grid declares the header grid own seven columns, cell for cell, and the
                     six data cells sit in them directly. It used to nest a six column grid inside
                     the click button, in a star column of a two column outer grid; that nesting
                     measured 20 pixels wider than the header at the same viewport, because the
                     inner grid stars were resolved against the outer star measure constraint rather
                     than against its arranged width, and the Name column came out 24 pixels wider
                     in a row than in the header. One column set, one width, nothing to reconcile.

                     The click target is a Button drawn under the cells rather than around them
                     (spec 12.2: clicking a row opens Target detail). A cell with no background of
                     its own is not hit tested, so a click anywhere in the row but on a badge
                     reaches the button.

                     It is a sibling of the row grid inside this Panel, not a child of the grid, so
                     it stretches the whole row including the seventh column and the trailing
                     inset. What keeps a click on Expand off it is z-order alone: the grid is
                     declared after the button and therefore paints and hit tests above it
                     (phase-review P3, correcting a comment that claimed the button stopped short
                     of the seventh column). A click in the sessions cell beside the button does
                     reach the row and opens the target, which is the row-wide behaviour spec 12.2
                     asks for. -->
                <Panel>
                  <!-- Phase 5 has no destination: the command raises TargetOpened and Phase 6
                       navigates (ruling Q17). It sits behind the cells rather than around them and
                       outside the row grid altogether: inside it, as a child spanning the six data
                       columns, it added about 20 pixels to the row grid star share that the header
                       grid did not have, and the Name column came out 24 pixels wider in a row than
                       in the header (measured). Behind the grid it takes part in no column at all.

                       IsTabStop is off and the automation name is the row's own (phase-review P3):
                       an empty, nameless, focusable Button per row put up to 250 unlabelled stops
                       in the tab order at the largest page size and announced "button" with
                       nothing in it. The keyboard reaches the row through its cells, which is what
                       the previous build's row did; the name is still set, so the click target is
                       addressable from outside and readable by a screen reader that walks the
                       tree rather than the tab order. -->
                  <Button Command="{Binding #Root.((dash:TargetListViewModel)DataContext).OpenTargetCommand}"
                          CommandParameter="{Binding}"
                          HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
                          VerticalAlignment="Stretch"
                          Background="Transparent"
                          BorderThickness="0"
                          IsTabStop="False"
                          AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding Name}"
                          MinHeight="0"
                          Padding="0" />

                <Grid x:Name="TargetRowGrid" MinHeight="34" Margin="{StaticResource TableTrailingInset}">
                  <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="*" MinWidth="120" />
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" SharedSizeGroup="{Binding #Root.DesignationSizeGroup}" />
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" SharedSizeGroup="{Binding #Root.((dash:TargetListViewModel)DataContext).Columns[2].SizeGroup}" />
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" SharedSizeGroup="{Binding #Root.IntegrationSizeGroup}" />
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" SharedSizeGroup="{Binding #Root.EquipmentSizeGroup}" />
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" SharedSizeGroup="{Binding #Root.LastSessionSizeGroup}" />
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" SharedSizeGroup="DashCustom" />
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" SharedSizeGroup="DashSessions" />
                  </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

                  <!-- One line, name then common name on the same baseline (spec 12.2, "The Name
                       cell"), not a stacked subtitle. The name takes the star column and trims with
                       an ellipsis; the common name sits beside it, capped and trimmed itself so its
                       own column cannot run away and push the name out of the cell. ClipToBounds is
                       the belt: neither text can reach the Designation column, which is what a name
                       longer than the cell used to do on the 120 target fixture
                       ("NGC 6960 - Veil Nebula,Filamentary Nebula,Western Veil"). -->
                  <Grid Grid.Column="0"
                        x:Name="NameCell"
                        MaxWidth="{Binding #Root.NameCellMaxWidth}"
                        HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                        ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*"
                        IsVisible="{Binding Columns[0].IsShown}"
                        ClipToBounds="True"
                        VerticalAlignment="Center">
                    <!-- Spec 12.2's mosaic link (Phase 18, ruling R19): straight after the name and
                         before the common name, a drawn 2 by 2 tile glyph in the accent ink. It
                         sits above the row's own click target, so it opens the mosaic and never
                         the row's Target detail. Docked first, so the DockPanel measures it before
                         the name and the name trims into what is left: a long name can never push
                         the link past the cell's clip edge. -->
                    <DockPanel Grid.Column="0"
                               MaxWidth="{Binding #Root.NameCellMaxWidth}"
                               HorizontalAlignment="Left">
                      <Button DockPanel.Dock="Right"
                              x:Name="MosaicLink"
                              Classes="quiet"
                              Margin="4,0,0,0"
                              Padding="2"
                              VerticalAlignment="Center"
                              IsVisible="{Binding HasMosaic}"
                              ToolTip.Tip="{Binding MosaicTooltip}"
                              AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding MosaicTooltip}"
                              Command="{Binding #Root.((dash:TargetListViewModel)DataContext).OpenMosaicCommand}"
                              CommandParameter="{Binding}">
                        <Path Width="14" Height="14" Stroke="{DynamicResource ColorAccent}" StrokeThickness="1.4" Data="M1,1 h5 v5 h-5 z M8,1 h5 v5 h-5 z M1,8 h5 v5 h-5 z M8,8 h5 v5 h-5 z" />
                      </Button>
                      <TextBlock Text="{Binding Name}"
                                 FontWeight="SemiBold"
                                 Margin="8,0,0,0"
                                 VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                 TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                                 Foreground="{DynamicResource ColorTextPrimary}" />
                    </DockPanel>
                    <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                               Text="{Binding CommonName}"
                               IsVisible="{Binding HasCommonName}"
                               Margin="6,0,8,0"
                               TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                               Foreground="{DynamicResource ColorTextSecondary}" />
                  </Grid>

                  <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                             x:Name="DesignationCell"
                             MaxWidth="{Binding #Root.DataCellMaxWidth}"
                             HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                             Classes="mono"
                             IsVisible="{Binding Columns[1].IsShown}"
                             Text="{Binding Designation}"
                             Padding="8,0"
                             VerticalAlignment="Center"
                             TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                             Foreground="{DynamicResource ColorTextSecondary}" />

                  <!-- One line of badges, never a WrapPanel: the wrap is what made the row two
                       lines high (user ruling U3 item 4). The column is shared and uncapped, so it
                       is as wide as the widest palette on the page and no badge is clipped and the
                       count is not capped (questions.md Q10); the two star columns give way to it
                       rather than a scroller carrying the row. -->
                  <ItemsControl Grid.Column="2"
                                x:Name="PaletteCell"
                                IsVisible="{Binding Columns[2].IsShown}"
                                Padding="8,0"
                                VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                ItemsSource="{Binding PaletteBadges}"
                                ItemTemplate="{StaticResource TargetFilterBadge}">
                    <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                      <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                        <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" />
                      </ItemsPanelTemplate>
                    </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                  </ItemsControl>

                  <TextBlock Grid.Column="3"
                             x:Name="IntegrationCell"
                             MaxWidth="{Binding #Root.DataCellMaxWidth}"
                             HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                             Classes="num"
                             IsVisible="{Binding Columns[3].IsShown}"
                             Text="{Binding IntegrationText}"
                             Padding="8,0"
                             VerticalAlignment="Center"
                             Foreground="{DynamicResource ColorTextPrimary}" />

                  <TextBlock Grid.Column="4"
                             x:Name="EquipmentCell"
                             MaxWidth="{Binding #Root.EquipmentCellMaxWidth}"
                             HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                             IsVisible="{Binding #Root.IsEquipmentShown}"
                             Text="{Binding EquipmentText}"
                             Padding="8,0"
                             VerticalAlignment="Center"
                             TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                             Foreground="{DynamicResource ColorTextSecondary}" />

                  <TextBlock Grid.Column="5"
                             x:Name="LastSessionCell"
                             MaxWidth="{Binding #Root.DataCellMaxWidth}"
                             HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                             Classes="num"
                             IsVisible="{Binding Columns[5].IsShown}"
                             Text="{Binding LastSessionText}"
                             Padding="8,0"
                             VerticalAlignment="Center"
                             Foreground="{DynamicResource ColorTextSecondary}" />

                  <!-- Spec 12.15's target-scope cells, after Last Session and before the sessions
                       control, in display order. Empty on an unresolved obj: group, which has no
                       target id to key a value by, and empty until the reader switches a column
                       on. -->
                  <ItemsControl Grid.Column="6"
                                x:Name="CustomCellStrip"
                                VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                ItemsSource="{Binding CustomCells}"
                                ItemTemplate="{StaticResource TargetCustomCell}">
                    <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                      <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                        <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" />
                      </ItemsPanelTemplate>
                    </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                  </ItemsControl>

                  <!-- The sessions control, at the row trailing end in the web application own form
                       (spec 12.2, user ruling U3 item 3): the word, not a chevron, and the session
                       count on the tooltip rather than as a label beside the name. -->
                  <Button Grid.Column="7"
                          x:Name="SessionsToggle"
                          Classes="sm"
                          Margin="8,0"
                          HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                          VerticalAlignment="Center"
                          Content="{Binding SessionsToggleText}"
                          Command="{Binding ToggleSessionsCommand}"
                          ToolTip.Tip="{Binding SessionsSummaryText}" />
                </Grid>
                </Panel>

                <!-- The per-session lines, inside the row's own rule pair. Realized only while the
                     row is open: a collapsed row costs no session line controls. No Border of its
                     own: DESIGN.md section 8 refuses nested bordered containers, so the indent and
                     the row's rules carry the containment and the 2 pixel left edge is retired. -->
                <ItemsControl x:Name="SessionsExpander"
                              IsVisible="{Binding IsExpanded}"
                              Margin="24,0,0,4"
                              ItemsSource="{Binding Sessions}">
                  <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                    <DataTemplate DataType="dash:SessionRowViewModel">
                      <!-- Spec 12.2's four session columns: Date, Frames, Integration, Filters,
                           and the Deep dive action. A shared-size grid rather than the
                           fixed-width StackPanel it was: the Filters cell's 200 pixel cap is what
                           wrapped a five filter night to two badge rows, and the four columns now
                           line up across nights for free. A fifth column is still one more cell
                           (task6.md 5.3). -->
                      <Grid MinHeight="26">
                        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                          <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" SharedSizeGroup="SessDate" />
                          <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" SharedSizeGroup="SessFrames" />
                          <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" SharedSizeGroup="SessIntegration" />
                          <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" SharedSizeGroup="SessFilters" />
                          <!-- Spec 12.15 amendment 2.4's session-scope cells, one more cell rather
                               than a layout change (task6.md 5.3's own rule). Ungated by any
                               picker (user choice 4), and read only when the row is expanded
                               (ruling C11), so the column measures nothing until then. -->
                          <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" SharedSizeGroup="SessCustom" />
                          <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                        </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                        <TextBlock Grid.Column="0"
                                   Classes="mono"
                                   Text="{Binding DateText}"
                                   Margin="8,0"
                                   VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                   Foreground="{DynamicResource ColorTextSecondary}" />
                        <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                                   Classes="num"
                                   Text="{Binding FrameCount}"
                                   Margin="8,0"
                                   VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                   Foreground="{DynamicResource ColorTextSecondary}" />
                        <TextBlock Grid.Column="2"
                                   Classes="num"
                                   Text="{Binding IntegrationText}"
                                   Margin="8,0"
                                   VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                   Foreground="{DynamicResource ColorTextSecondary}" />
                        <!-- The same Border.tag badges the Palette column draws (spec 12.2), the
                             shared TargetFilterBadge template above, in the same alphabetical
                             order. A night whose frames carry no filter renders nothing here
                             rather than a placeholder: the ItemsControl is simply empty. -->
                        <ItemsControl Grid.Column="3"
                                      x:Name="SessionFiltersCell"
                                      Margin="8,0"
                                      VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                      ItemsSource="{Binding Filters}"
                                      ItemTemplate="{StaticResource TargetFilterBadge}">
                          <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                            <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" />
                            </ItemsPanelTemplate>
                          </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                        </ItemsControl>
                        <ItemsControl Grid.Column="4"
                                      x:Name="SessionCustomCellStrip"
                                      VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                      ItemsSource="{Binding CustomCells}"
                                      ItemTemplate="{StaticResource TargetCustomCell}">
                          <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                            <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" />
                            </ItemsPanelTemplate>
                          </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                        </ItemsControl>

                        <!-- Spec 12.2's Deep dive action: the row's group key and this line's
                             own date, and nothing else (task6.md 6.3). Works identically for an
                             unresolved obj: group, whose GroupKey already carries that prefix. -->
                        <Button Grid.Column="5"
                                x:Name="DeepDiveButton"
                                Content="Deep dive"
                                Classes="sm"
                                Margin="8,0"
                                VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                Command="{Binding #Root.((dash:TargetListViewModel)DataContext).DeepDiveCommand}"
                                CommandParameter="{Binding}" />
                      </Grid>
                    </DataTemplate>
                  </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                </ItemsControl>
              </StackPanel>
            </Border>
          </DataTemplate>
        </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
      </ItemsControl>
    </ScrollViewer>
  </Grid>
</UserControl>
